  <static seed="660" source="Library/Michigan/Chap18Sec1/Q17.pg">
    <statement><p>If <m>C</m> is the <m>z</m>-axis from the origin to the point
<m>\left(0,0,2\right)</m>, then</p>
<p><m>\int_{C}(-2 \vec j -2 \vec k)\cdot d\vec r =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>2\cdot \left(-2\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>z</m>-axis, only the
<m>\vec k</m> component of
the vector field contributes to the integral:
<me>\int_C(-2 \vec j -2 \vec k)\cdot d\vec r =
\int_C -2\vec k\cdot d\vec r =
-2\cdot \mbox{ Length of } C =
-2\cdot 2 = -4.</me>



</solution>
